    "lawCode": "Amd §§190, 210-B, 606 & 1511, Tax L",
    "lawSection": "Tax Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S1453\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the tax law, in relation to\nincreasing the tax credits for premiums paid for long-term care\ninsurance\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to provide additional tax credit\ninducements to encourage individuals to either retain long term care\n(LTC) insurance coverage or encourage more, currently non enrolled\nindividuals, to purchase long term care (LTC) insurance.\n\nThe LTC insurance market has recently become much more unstable due\nto: a) significant reductions in the number of individuals that have\npurchased coverage or who choose to continue to retain such coverage,\nb) a significant reduction in the number of insurance carriers that\noffer LTC insurance, and c)the approximately 50% increase in LTC\ninsurance premiums that has been granted in 2015 and the likely\npossibility of vast increases being imposed in policy years 2016 and\nbeyond.\n\nThis enhanced tax credit is available for the next three years and\napplies retroactively from January 1, 2017 to December 31, 2021 so\nthat New York State will have the sufficient time needed to develop\nand enact new methodologies to help stabilize the currently unstable\nand volatile LTC insurance market.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1: Amends Tax Law section 190 to increase the corporate income\ntax credit for purchasing long term care insurance from 20% of the\npremium paid to 40%. This enhanced tax credit shall be applicable for\npremiums paid from January 1, 2017 to January 1, 2021.\n\nSection 2: Amends Tax Law section 210-B to increase the franchise\nincome tax credit incentive for the computation income tax from 20% to\n40% of the premium paid for LTC insurance.\n\nSection 3: Amends Tax Law section 606 to increase the personal income\ntax credit from 20% to 40% of the cost of premiums paid to purchase\nLTC insurance.\n\nSection 4: Amends Tax Law section 1511 to add the enhanced income tax\ncredit for insurance franchisees from 20% to 40% of the cost of\npremiums for LTC insurance.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe purpose of this bill is to temporarily enhance the current 20% LTC\npremium income tax credit to 40%, retroactively to January 1, 2015.\nThe underlying goal of this bill is to help to offset recently\napproved LTC insurance premium rate increases for the next upcoming\nthree years. Some of the recently approved LTC insurance rates\nincreased by over 50%. Many current LTC policy holders are over the\nage of 55 and cannot afford this drastic premium rate increase.  The\nenhanced tax credit from 20% to 40% will give temporary relief to\nthose that currently have LTC insurance, help to stabilize the market,\nand encourage younger healthier lives to think about purchasing LTC\npolicies which will help curtail any additional future premium rate\nincreases. This bill, by increasing the current 20% LTC premium income\ntax credit to 40%, retroactively, to January 1, 2015, will help to\noffset most or all of the recently approved LTC insurance rate\nincreases granted to insurers by the Department of Financial Services\n(a three year policy period) for the next three years.\n\nThis measure's provisions sunset in three years on 12/31/18. The\nreason for the three year sunset is to give sufficient time for the\nLegislature and Governor to develop and implement new methodologies to\nhelp stabilize the LTC insurance market over the long term. While this\nbill does double the income tax credit for those premiums paid by\ninsureds, it should not be a significant fiscal hit on the State\nBudget. This is because with the enactment of this measure, many\ncovered persons will continue to keep their LTC policies. If, however,\nthis measure is not enacted, many LTC policy insureds will let such\npolicies laps and should that occur, any long term care or nursing\nhome care required by such persons would not be financed by private\ninsurance carriers, but by public Medicaid payments that are supplied\nby State and local governments.\n\nAs the general population becomes older, more and more individuals are\nlooking for affordable ways to finance their nursing home and long\nterm care needs. While there are available policies that provide\npayment for long term care, more products should be authorized under\nNew York law so that consumers will have more types of insurance\ncoverage available to meet their specific needs or the needs of their\nfamily and to increase the selection of adult care facilities such\ninsureds may go to.\n\nIn addition, encouraging more individuals to finance their own long\nterm care, will help to curtail future expenditures from the State's\nMedicaid program, which currently finances the lion's share of the\nprovision of long term care in this State.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nIt is the intent of this bill to stabilize the LTC insurance market so\nthat it can remain an important financing mechanism to finance end of\nlife, home health, and nursing home care. This, in the end, should\nhelp to decrease State Medicaid expenditures.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on January 1, 2017.\n",
